/* ============================================================================
   prime-factors-demo.epher -- the prime factorization of several numbers

   Prints the factors builtin for a few numbers. Every integer greater
   than 1 factors uniquely into primes (the fundamental theorem of
   arithmetic), and factors spells that factorization with exponents:
   factors(360) is the display string "2^3 * 3^2 * 5". The script prints
   the strings (they are display strings: readable, not arithmetic), and
   multiplies the 360 case back out with plain arithmetic to show the
   factorization really is 360.

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 1, the fundamental theorem of arithmetic (the unique factorization
   theorem). factors is exact for every integer the calculator can hold.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/primes/prime-factors-demo.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/primes/prime-factors-demo.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- factorizations --------------------------------------------------------------
factors(360)                    // 2^3 * 3^2 * 5: 360 = 8 * 9 * 5
factors(1024)                   // a pure power: 2^10
factors(2026)                   // 2 * 1013, both prime
factors(97)                     // a prime factors to itself
factors(60)                     // 2^2 * 3 * 5
factors(1)                      // 1 has no prime factors and shows as 1

// ---- multiply the 360 factorization back out -------------------------------------
2 ^ 3 * 3 ^ 2 * 5               // the factors of 360, multiplied out
// And the exponent law for counting divisors: with exponents a, b, c the
// divisor count is (a + 1)(b + 1)(c + 1), so 360 should have 24 divisors.
(3 + 1) * (2 + 1) * (1 + 1)     // the divisor-count formula for 360
ndivisors(360)                  // the builtin agrees
/* ---- expected output ----
= 2^3 * 3^2 * 5
= 2^10
= 2 * 1013
= 97
= 2^2 * 3 * 5
= 1
= 360
= 24
= 24
*/
